+---
+node: previews
+---
+
+# Decision: Cache replacement policy for the preview worker's private cache
+
+## Context
+
+The preview worker renders one JPEG contact sheet per clip revision and renderer version, and keeps them in a private cache bounded at 12 GiB. Twenty-four editors work against an archive of roughly 90,000 clips, viewing about 6,000 distinct clips each week. A sheet is typically 2 MiB and takes about 450 ms to render; a cache miss is answered with a pending ticket that the browser retries every two seconds.
+
+Editors alternate two very different rhythms. A screening pass moves through a reel and touches each sheet once. A comparison session returns to the same handful of sheets many times, and it is the comparison that the product exists to support. Both rhythms draw on the same bounded cache, and neither the public endpoint nor the internal preview interface can express a preference: the cache is private to the worker, no node pins entries, and no node observes replacement state. The replacement policy is therefore the only instrument that decides what survives a screening pass.
+
+The stakes are asymmetric. If a screening pass displaces the comparison sheets, the editor re-renders them — extra render work at the peak of a session — and the comparison that was in flight has to be rebuilt. If the policy errs the other way and retains sheets nobody wants, the waste is bounded bytes and some bookkeeping.
+
+Three constraints are inherited rather than chosen here, and every option below respects them: the preview interface continues to return only 200, 202, or 404; the render queue continues to run at most four concurrent jobs; and all replacement metadata stays inside the worker. Allowing callers to pin a sheet was considered and is excluded by those same constraints — the interface carries no pin channel — which is precisely why the policy must carry the whole burden.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+### Gates
+
+- **Comparison sheets must survive a one-off screening pass.** A policy that lets a pass over sheets, each seen once, displace the sheets an editor is actively comparing fails regardless of its other qualities.
+- **The policy must hold no per-request state that outlives a sheet's usefulness.** Retention decisions must not depend on information that survives indefinitely after the work it describes has ended.
+
+### Weighted criteria
+
+- **Protection of comparison sheets under scan load** *(major)* — the probability that the sheets an editor is comparing remain cached through a screening pass. Turns on the tenet *Protect repeatedly compared previews over one-pass screening scans when cache space is contended.*
+- **Bookkeeping cost** *(moderate)* — the per-entry state and per-request work the policy requires of the worker.
+- **Adaptation when the working set changes** *(moderate)* — how quickly the policy stops protecting work that is no longer wanted, such as when editors switch productions.
+- **Preservation of reuse information across eviction** *(moderate)* — whether the policy remembers that a sheet was returned to, after the sheet itself is gone.
+- **Operational tuning burden** *(minor-to-moderate)* — how many independent parameters the pilot must tune, and how legibly a mis-tuned policy fails.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### LRU over all cached sheets
+
+**Description.** Every cached sheet sits in one ordered collection. Each entry carries the time it was last handed back. A cache access refreshes that timestamp, and admitting a new sheet evicts the entries with the oldest timestamps until the sheet fits within the 12 GiB bound. This is the standard bounded-cache answer, and it is genuinely attractive here: one timestamp per sheet, no separate structures, no parameters beyond the byte bound itself, and a working set that changes is followed immediately because the next request simply establishes the new recency order.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- *Comparison sheets must survive a one-off screening pass* — **eliminated.** During a screening pass each sheet is accessed exactly once, and every access makes that sheet the most recent in the collection. The comparison sheets' timestamps age out relative to the pass's traffic, and a long enough pass replaces them.
+- *The policy must hold no per-request state that outlives a sheet's usefulness* — **passes.** Recency is refreshed on access and carries no history.
+- *Protection of comparison sheets under scan load* — **weak.** Recency cannot distinguish a sheet returned to twice from one touched once during a pass.
+- *Bookkeeping cost* — **strong.** One timestamp per entry and an ordered structure; no counter maintenance, no background sweep.
+- *Adaptation when the working set changes* — **strong.** The first request after a change sets the new recency order.
+- *Preservation of reuse information across eviction* — **weak.** An evicted sheet leaves no trace; nothing distinguishes it from a sheet that was never wanted.
+- *Operational tuning burden* — **strong.** The 12 GiB bound is the only knob.
+
+**Summary.** The cheapest policy to run, and the one that most directly sacrifices comparison sheets to a screening pass.
+
+### Frequency counting with aging
+
+**Description.** Each cached sheet carries a count of how many times it has been retrieved. Counts are periodically aged — decayed toward zero on a schedule, so that a sheet's popularity reflects recent work rather than all-time work. Eviction removes the sheet with the lowest count, using recency to break ties. The intuition is direct: count what editors actually return to, and age the counts so that yesterday's favorites do not crowd out today's work. It reaches for scan resistance without multiple queues.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- *Comparison sheets must survive a one-off screening pass* — **passes.** A pass does not raise the counters of comparison sheets, so those sheets keep the higher counts and are evicted last.
+- *The policy must hold no per-request state that outlives a sheet's usefulness* — **passes.** Aging decays counts on a schedule, so popularity does not persist indefinitely.
+- *Protection of comparison sheets under scan load* — **strong.** The counters are precisely the reuse signal the policy needs.
+- *Bookkeeping cost* — **partial.** A counter per entry plus a periodic decay pass over every cached entry, which is work the other options do not perform at all.
+- *Adaptation when the working set changes* — **weak.** Counts decay only as fast as the aging schedule allows; a production switch leaves the previous production's popular sheets protected for that whole horizon. Choosing the horizon is exactly the judgment this option exists to avoid making.
+- *Preservation of reuse information across eviction* — **partial.** Counts live with the sheet, so a sheet evicted for being unpopular carries no record of the reuse that once justified it.
+- *Operational tuning burden* — **partial.** The aging horizon and the decay schedule are both parameters, and a badly chosen horizon produces either stale protection or no protection — failures that are hard to distinguish from one another in production.
+
+**Summary.** Strong reuse signal bought with counter maintenance and an aging horizon whose mis-tuning is hard to diagnose.
+
+### 2Q
+
+**Description.** Three structures. The first-use queue holds sheets that have never been retrieved since admission, ordered by admission and evicted oldest-first. The hot queue holds sheets retrieved at least twice, ordered by recency and evicted least-recently-retrieved first. The ghost queue holds preview keys only — no JPEG data — for sheets that leave the hot queue; a later request for a ghosted key re-renders the sheet and re-enters it directly into the hot queue rather than having to earn promotion a second time. The 12 GiB budget is split between the two data queues, and each queue evicts within itself when its own share is exceeded, so a screening pass cannot empty the hot queue to make room for scan traffic.
+
+Two properties of the option are worth stating precisely, because both are easy to get wrong in an implementation. First, reuse is counted in retrievals from the cache, not in requests: the retrieval that first delivers a freshly rendered sheet is its delivery and not reuse, and the browser's two-second retries of a pending ticket are not reuse either. Counting requests instead would promote nearly every sheet into the hot queue and collapse the policy into a single queue. Second, the ghost queue is what makes re-entry cheap; without it, a returning sheet would re-enter the scan buffer and would need to be retrieved twice again before it was protected.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- *Comparison sheets must survive a one-off screening pass* — **passes.** A pass lands entirely in the first-use queue and is evicted in admission order; the hot queue is untouched by scan traffic.
+- *The policy must hold no per-request state that outlives a sheet's usefulness* — **passes.** Ghost keys hold no sheet data and are bounded in number; a sheet that stops being requested leaves the hot queue by recency once the hot share needs the space.
+- *Protection of comparison sheets under scan load* — **strong.** The hot queue holds comparison sheets in a space the scan cannot draw on, and the ghost queue preserves their reuse standing across eviction.
+- *Bookkeeping cost* — **partial.** Three structures, per-entry state, per-key ghost records, a byte split, and the retrieval-versus-request distinction to implement correctly.
+- *Adaptation when the working set changes* — **partial.** A sheet that stops being requested stays in the hot queue until the hot share evicts it by recency, and ghost keys outlive their sheets until the ghost depth is reached. Adaptation is slower than LRU's, though it does not need an aging horizon to be set.
+- *Preservation of reuse information across eviction* — **strong.** The ghost queue exists for exactly this purpose, and it is the only option that provides it.
+- *Operational tuning burden* — **weak.** The policy carries three parameters — the budget split, the ghost depth, and the reuse counting rule — and the split has to be tuned against observed screening patterns. A mis-tuned split shows up as scan traffic displacing hot sheets or as hot budget going unused, and both are visible from queue occupancy.
+
+**Summary.** The strongest protection for reused sheets, paid for in bookkeeping and in parameters that must be tuned.
+
+## Selection
+
+2Q is selected. It is the only option that passes the comparison-sheet gate while also preserving reuse information across eviction, and it is the only one whose protection comes without a horizon that has to be set: the aged-frequency option protects comparison sheets too, but by accepting a period during which the previous production's favorites are still protected, which fails the adaptation criterion and adds a parameter whose mis-tuning is hard to read from production behavior. LRU is eliminated outright by the gate — not by being less good on the criteria, but by failing the property the policy exists to provide.
+
+The selection turns on the tenet *Protect repeatedly compared previews over one-pass screening scans when cache space is contended*. That tenet is the project's standing answer to a question no single requirement settles, and it is what makes protecting comparison sheets outrank the cheaper bookkeeping the other two options offer.
+
+Committing to 2Q forecloses the simplest cache this component could have had. A future reader who believes the three structures are unjustified has to argue against that tenet rather than against a preference for elegance, and any future move to a one-queue policy is a reversal of this decision rather than a simplification of it. The split between the two data queues and the depth of the ghost queue are parameters to be tuned against pilot behavior, not fixed values, and tuning them does not reopen the choice of policy.
+
+Committing also obliges downstream: the reuse signal must be counted in retrievals rather than requests or the protection this decision buys is lost while the bookkeeping remains; admission must be serialized so concurrent render completions cannot push cached data past the bound; and the queue state, counters, and configuration stay inside the worker, invisible to the review API and the browser. The obligation to count retrievals rather than requests is the one most likely to be lost to a well-meaning simplification, because the simpler implementation — increment a counter on every request — looks correct and quietly disables the policy.
