 ## Private Cache
 
-Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
+Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+## Cache Replacement Policy
+
+The cache uses 2Q: a two-queue approximation of LIRS that separates sheets seen once from sheets seen more than once. The worker keeps three structures — two queues that hold JPEG data and one queue of keys that holds no data at all.
+
+| Structure | Holds | Order | Eviction |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| First-use queue | JPEG data of sheets never retrieved since admission | Admission order | Oldest admission first |
+| Hot queue | JPEG data of sheets retrieved at least twice since admission | Least-recently-retrieved first | Least-recently-retrieved first |
+| Ghost queue | Preview keys of sheets evicted from the hot queue — no JPEG data | Record order | Oldest recorded key first |
+
+```
+   render completes
+          │
+          ▼
+  ┌───────────────┐   2nd+ retrieval   ┌─────────────┐
+  │ first-use     │───────────────────►│ hot         │
+  │ queue         │                    │ queue       │
+  └───────┬───────┘                    └──────┬──────┘
+          │ evict oldest admission            │ evict least-recently-retrieved
+          ▼                                   ▼
+     (bytes freed)                   ┌─────────────┐
+                                     │ ghost queue │  keys only, no JPEG
+                                     └─────────────┘
+          ▲                                   │
+          └────── ghost hit: render again, ────┘
+                 admit straight to hot
+```
+
+### Admission and Promotion
+
+A rendered sheet enters the first-use queue, unless its preview key is already recorded in the ghost queue — a key in the ghost queue proves the sheet was retrieved at least twice before, so the re-rendered sheet is admitted directly to the hot queue. A preview key is held by at most one of the three structures at any time.
+
+The reuse signal counts retrievals from the cache, not requests. The retrieval that first delivers a freshly rendered sheet is its delivery and is not reuse; every retrieval after that one is reuse. A sheet in the first-use queue that is retrieved again is promoted to the hot queue without being re-rendered, and its byte accounting moves with it. A sheet in the hot queue that is retrieved has its position within the hot queue refreshed.
+
+This keeps one-off screening scans in the first-use queue, where they are evicted in admission order, while sheets an editor keeps coming back to accumulate in the hot queue and survive the scans. Render jobs and pending-job responses are not reuse signals; a browser retrying a pending ticket every two seconds does not turn a one-off sheet into a hot entry.
+
+### Budgets
+
+The 12 GiB cached-JPEG budget is partitioned between the two data queues. Each queue evicts within itself when its own budget is exceeded, so the hot queue is never emptied to make room for a first-use sheet.
+
+| Parameter | Value | Meaning |
+|---|---|---|
+| Total cached JPEG | 12 GiB | Hard ceiling on cached JPEG data |
+| First-use budget | 3 GiB | Bytes of sheets that have never been retrieved since admission |
+| Hot budget | 9 GiB | Bytes of sheets retrieved at least twice |
+| Ghost queue depth | 4,096 keys | Preview keys remembered after hot eviction |
+
+At the typical 2 MiB sheet, the first-use budget holds roughly 1,500 sheets and the hot budget roughly 4,600. The split is a tuned parameter: the pilot adjusts it from observed screening patterns, and the two budgets always sum to 12 GiB. A sheet larger than the first-use budget is admitted to the hot queue instead; a sheet larger than the hot budget is served but not cached.
+
+### Concurrency and Privacy
+
+Admission is serialized inside the worker, so concurrently completing render jobs cannot push cached JPEG data past 12 GiB. Queue membership, recency order, ghost keys, and budget configuration are worker-internal state: the internal preview endpoint exposes only 200, 202, and 404, and no other node observes them.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache replacement policy | 2Q — first-use queue, hot queue, and ghost queue (see `decisions/cache-replacement-policy.md`) | LRU; frequency counting with aging | Editors alternate long sequential screenings with repeated comparisons of a small set of clips. LRU lets a screening scan evict comparison sheets, and aged frequency counters keep yesterday's favorites alive well past their usefulness. 2Q protects reused sheets from one-off scans, and the extra bookkeeping is confined to the worker, where the cache is private. |
+| Eviction order within a queue | First-use queue by admission order, hot queue by least-recently-retrieved | Admission order in both queues; least-recently-used everywhere | The first-use queue is a scan buffer where age is the only signal available; ordering it by recency would need bookkeeping the queue does not justify. The hot queue holds sheets whose usefulness tracks how recently they were used, so recency ordering protects the working set. |
+| Reuse signal | Retrievals from the cache, excluding the delivery of a freshly rendered sheet | Counting every request, including pending-job retries | The browser retries a pending ticket every two seconds. Counting those retries would promote nearly every sheet to the hot queue and collapse the policy into a single queue, so reuse is counted only where a sheet is actually handed back from the cache. |
+| Budget split | 3 GiB first-use, 9 GiB hot, summing to 12 GiB | Even split; a first-use-weighted split | The hot queue holds the comparison sheets the policy exists to protect, and the first-use queue only buffers scans, so the hot queue gets the larger share. Both budgets are pilot-tuned. |
+| Ghost queue depth | 4,096 keys | Proportional to cache size; unbounded | A fixed key count bounds the metadata a worker restart or a long screening can accumulate, and 4,096 keys covers the comparison set of several editors at once. |
+| Ghost hit handling | Re-render, then admit straight to the hot queue | Re-render into the first-use queue | A ghost key records prior reuse, so the sheet returns to the hot queue it was evicted from rather than re-entering the scan buffer and having to earn promotion again. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+
+1. The replacement policy is 2Q, with the split between the first-use and hot queues treated as a pilot-tuned parameter.
+2. The public and internal preview interfaces carry no cache state, so the policy remains invisible to the review API and the browser.
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Whether the hot queue should also order by frequency once pilot telemetry shows that some sheets are retrieved many more times than others.
+2. Whether the preview cache should survive a worker restart, and if so how the queues are rebuilt.
+3. Whether the ghost queue depth needs to grow with the number of editors on the worker.
+
+## References
+
